This directory is organized around several related mathematical roles.
There is no single main function and no single module that everything flows through.

A useful reading order is:

    1. state.py
       The shortest view of a complete hydrogen state:
           bound_state = radial_mode * angular_mode

    2. radial.py
       How the radius-dependent part is constructed.

    3. angular.py
       How the angle-dependent part is constructed.

    4. equations.py
       How radial, angular, and complete states are checked against
       their differential equations.

    5. inner_products.py
       Normalization and orthogonality operations.

    6. catalogue.py
       Which allowed (n, l, m) combinations are generated.

Supporting files:

    quantum_numbers.py
        Rules for allowed n, l, and m values.

    coordinates.py
        Shared radius and angle symbols.

    symbolic.py
        Shared symbolic simplification.

How the surrounding program reaches this code:

    hydrogen_states.py
        Compatibility facade. Existing scripts import the old public names
        from here; it forwards those names into the smaller modules.

    check_hydrogen_states.py
        Gets the state catalogues and asks equations.py to verify the radial,
        angular, and full stationary equations.

    export_polar_f64.py
        Gets the state catalogue, calls bound_state(), and converts each
        symbolic state into the generated runtime representation.

    export_polar_f64_receipts.py
        Evaluates the same generated states at sample points for runtime checks.

    derive.py
        A separate derivation/explanation path showing where some of the
        mathematical forms come from. It is not the runtime call path.

One useful dependency sketch is:

    check_hydrogen_states.py
        -> catalogue.py
        -> equations.py
             -> state.py
                  -> radial.py
                  -> angular.py

    export_polar_f64.py
        -> catalogue.py
        -> state.py
             -> radial.py
             -> angular.py

So state.py is a reasonable first concrete file to read, but it is not the
center of the whole package.
